1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,830
Saturday morning.

2
00:00:01,830 --> 00:00:04,740
An open house in an ordinary suburb.

3
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:05,789
Three bedrooms,

4
00:00:05,789 --> 00:00:06,569
one bath,

5
00:00:06,569 --> 00:00:08,721
a lawn the size of a parking

6
00:00:08,721 --> 00:00:09,590
space.

7
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:11,507
Someone just offered cash,

8
00:00:11,507 --> 00:00:13,510
over the asking price.

9
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:14,340
And Maya,

10
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:16,292
who has saved for years,

11
00:00:16,292 --> 00:00:18,555
is holding up a sign that just

12
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:19,092
says,

13
00:00:19,092 --> 00:00:19,975
please.

14
00:00:20,217 --> 00:00:22,397
Here's the strange part.

15
00:00:22,617 --> 00:00:25,277
Americans have never earned more.

16
00:00:25,297 --> 00:00:27,546
The typical household made a record

17
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:29,590
eighty-seven thousand four hundred and

18
00:00:29,590 --> 00:00:31,000
sixty dollars last year,

19
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,827
says the Census Bureau.

20
00:00:32,847 --> 00:00:35,123
But the typical existing home sold for

21
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:37,192
four hundred and sixteen thousand seven

22
00:00:37,192 --> 00:00:39,447
hundred dollars in October.

23
00:00:39,517 --> 00:00:41,890
And with mortgage rates above seven

24
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:42,578
percent,

25
00:00:42,578 --> 00:00:45,188
buying it takes a paycheck most households

26
00:00:45,188 --> 00:00:46,737
simply don't have.

27
00:00:46,983 --> 00:00:49,812
So why can't Americans afford a home

28
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:51,183
anymore?

29
00:00:51,313 --> 00:00:52,973
Let's follow the money.

30
00:00:53,193 --> 00:00:55,390
The price that outran the paycheck,

31
00:00:55,390 --> 00:00:57,833
the rate that rode a rollercoaster,

32
00:00:57,833 --> 00:00:59,536
the owners who can't move,

33
00:00:59,536 --> 00:01:01,486
the homes we never built,

34
00:01:01,486 --> 00:01:04,061
and the room where it's decided.

35
00:01:19,317 --> 00:01:22,277
Start with the simplest test there is.

36
00:01:22,437 --> 00:01:24,424
Harvard's Joint Center for Housing Studies

37
00:01:24,424 --> 00:01:27,008
divides the price of a typical existing

38
00:01:27,008 --> 00:01:29,193
single-family home by the typical

39
00:01:29,193 --> 00:01:30,787
household's income.

40
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:32,675
In the nineteen-nineties,

41
00:01:32,675 --> 00:01:34,210
a home cost about three point two

42
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:35,577
years of income.

43
00:01:35,617 --> 00:01:37,150
By twenty nineteen,

44
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:38,317
four point one.

45
00:01:38,317 --> 00:01:39,799
In twenty twenty-two,

46
00:01:39,799 --> 00:01:41,687
an all-time high of five.

47
00:01:41,707 --> 00:01:43,604
And in twenty twenty-five,

48
00:01:43,604 --> 00:01:45,404
still four point seven.

49
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:48,370
It's not that paychecks stood still.

50
00:01:48,630 --> 00:01:50,488
The Census Bureau says real median

51
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:52,758
household income reached a record in

52
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:54,350
twenty twenty-five.

53
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:56,180
But prices sprinted.

54
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:58,555
Harvard says home prices nationwide rose

55
00:01:58,555 --> 00:02:01,530
fifty-four percent since twenty twenty.

56
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:03,380
The paycheck jogged.

57
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:05,977
The price tag took the elevator.

58
00:02:06,217 --> 00:02:08,391
And the classic starter home quietly

59
00:02:08,391 --> 00:02:09,657
shrank out of reach.

60
00:02:09,657 --> 00:02:11,403
In March twenty nineteen,

61
00:02:11,403 --> 00:02:14,275
about forty-nine percent of homes for sale

62
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:16,927
were affordable to a household earning

63
00:02:16,927 --> 00:02:19,737
seventy-five thousand dollars or less.

64
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:21,991
By March twenty twenty-six,

65
00:02:21,991 --> 00:02:24,067
just twenty-three percent.

66
00:02:24,157 --> 00:02:26,064
Less than one in four.

67
00:02:26,317 --> 00:02:27,907
Let me draw it.

68
00:02:27,977 --> 00:02:29,575
One bar for each period,

69
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:31,977
measured in years of income.

70
00:02:31,977 --> 00:02:32,976
The nineties,

71
00:02:32,976 --> 00:02:34,187
about three.

72
00:02:34,197 --> 00:02:35,304
Twenty nineteen,

73
00:02:35,304 --> 00:02:36,467
about four.

74
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:38,357
The twenty twenty-two peak,

75
00:02:38,357 --> 00:02:39,187
five.

76
00:02:39,237 --> 00:02:40,661
Twenty twenty-five,

77
00:02:40,661 --> 00:02:42,227
four point seven.

78
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:44,933
Every extra step is roughly one more

79
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:47,632
year of a whole household's pay.

80
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:54,973
Now the part that really hurts.

81
00:02:54,973 --> 00:02:56,827
Almost nobody pays cash,

82
00:02:56,827 --> 00:02:58,469
so the real price of a home

83
00:02:58,469 --> 00:02:59,763
is the loan.

84
00:02:59,763 --> 00:03:01,778
Freddie Mac tracks the average thirty-year

85
00:03:01,778 --> 00:03:04,393
fixed mortgage rate every week.

86
00:03:04,453 --> 00:03:06,946
In January twenty twenty-one it hit a

87
00:03:06,946 --> 00:03:08,760
record low of two point six five

88
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:09,813
percent.

89
00:03:10,403 --> 00:03:12,225
Before the end of twenty twenty-two it

90
00:03:12,225 --> 00:03:13,493
was above seven.

91
00:03:13,493 --> 00:03:16,123
This October it climbed back to seven

92
00:03:16,123 --> 00:03:17,294
point four percent,

93
00:03:17,294 --> 00:03:19,207
and last week it was still seven

94
00:03:19,207 --> 00:03:21,002
point one one percent.

95
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:23,153
Here's what that does to one house,

96
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:25,170
by our own calculation.

97
00:03:25,170 --> 00:03:27,002
Take October's median price,

98
00:03:27,002 --> 00:03:28,603
put twenty percent down,

99
00:03:28,603 --> 00:03:31,290
and borrow the rest for thirty years.

100
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:32,883
At two point six five percent,

101
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:35,256
principal and interest come to about one

102
00:03:35,256 --> 00:03:37,432
thousand three hundred and forty dollars a

103
00:03:37,432 --> 00:03:38,230
month.

104
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:40,359
At seven point one one percent,

105
00:03:40,359 --> 00:03:42,780
about two thousand two hundred and forty

106
00:03:42,780 --> 00:03:43,780
dollars.

107
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:44,620
Same house,

108
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:45,751
same kitchen,

109
00:03:45,751 --> 00:03:47,430
same tiny lawn.

110
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:49,328
about nine hundred dollars more,

111
00:03:49,328 --> 00:03:50,373
every month,

112
00:03:50,373 --> 00:03:51,826
for thirty years.

113
00:03:52,083 --> 00:03:54,413
Let me draw the income test.

114
00:03:54,413 --> 00:03:56,183
The realtors' rule of thumb says the

115
00:03:56,183 --> 00:03:57,953
mortgage payment shouldn't be more than a

116
00:03:57,953 --> 00:03:59,733
quarter of your income.

117
00:03:59,793 --> 00:04:00,996
At today's payment,

118
00:04:00,996 --> 00:04:03,035
that means earning about one hundred and

119
00:04:03,035 --> 00:04:05,063
eight thousand dollars a year.

120
00:04:05,323 --> 00:04:07,183
Now the median household.

121
00:04:07,183 --> 00:04:08,954
eighty-seven thousand four hundred and

122
00:04:08,954 --> 00:04:10,263
sixty dollars.

123
00:04:10,363 --> 00:04:11,147
Two bars,

124
00:04:11,147 --> 00:04:11,931
one gap,

125
00:04:11,931 --> 00:04:14,963
and that gap is the whole story.

126
00:04:14,993 --> 00:04:16,719
This is our calculation,

127
00:04:16,719 --> 00:04:19,068
before taxes and insurance.

128
00:04:19,317 --> 00:04:21,932
The National Association of Realtors runs

129
00:04:21,932 --> 00:04:24,057
the same test every month.

130
00:04:24,117 --> 00:04:26,897
Its Housing Affordability Index equals one

131
00:04:26,897 --> 00:04:29,081
hundred when a family on the median

132
00:04:29,081 --> 00:04:31,663
income has exactly enough to qualify for

133
00:04:31,663 --> 00:04:32,636
the median home,

134
00:04:32,636 --> 00:04:34,427
with twenty percent down.

135
00:04:34,707 --> 00:04:36,919
In October it read one hundred point

136
00:04:36,919 --> 00:04:37,560
seven,

137
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:39,551
down from one hundred and seven point

138
00:04:39,551 --> 00:04:41,257
three a year earlier.

139
00:04:41,417 --> 00:04:43,162
Barely over the line,

140
00:04:43,162 --> 00:04:44,929
and only if you have the down

141
00:04:44,929 --> 00:04:45,970
payment.

142
00:04:46,217 --> 00:04:46,950
Harvard,

143
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:49,198
adding taxes and insurance,

144
00:04:49,198 --> 00:04:51,567
puts the bar even higher.

145
00:04:51,637 --> 00:04:54,049
The income needed for the median-priced

146
00:04:54,049 --> 00:04:56,242
home was one hundred and twenty thousand

147
00:04:56,242 --> 00:04:58,215
eight hundred dollars at the end of

148
00:04:58,215 --> 00:04:59,509
twenty twenty-five,

149
00:04:59,509 --> 00:05:01,701
up from sixty-eight thousand seven hundred

150
00:05:01,701 --> 00:05:03,617
dollars five years earlier.

151
00:05:03,667 --> 00:05:04,900
In twenty twenty,

152
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:07,407
only thirty-one metro areas required more

153
00:05:07,407 --> 00:05:10,097
than a hundred thousand dollars a year.

154
00:05:10,137 --> 00:05:11,845
By twenty twenty-five,

155
00:05:11,845 --> 00:05:14,426
one hundred and sixty-nine did.

156
00:05:18,883 --> 00:05:21,244
High rates did something stranger,

157
00:05:21,244 --> 00:05:22,003
too.

158
00:05:22,003 --> 00:05:24,883
They froze the people who already own.

159
00:05:24,883 --> 00:05:25,309
Meet Rex,

160
00:05:25,309 --> 00:05:25,993
Maya's dad.

161
00:05:25,993 --> 00:05:29,318
Rex refinanced when rates were near the

162
00:05:29,318 --> 00:05:30,523
bottom.

163
00:05:30,563 --> 00:05:32,463
And he is not alone.

164
00:05:32,463 --> 00:05:33,960
According to Harvard,

165
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,346
half of all outstanding mortgages carried

166
00:05:36,346 --> 00:05:38,516
rates below four percent at the end

167
00:05:38,516 --> 00:05:40,417
of twenty twenty-five.

168
00:05:40,683 --> 00:05:41,954
So if Rex sells,

169
00:05:41,954 --> 00:05:44,030
he gives up a cheap loan and

170
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:46,105
takes a new one at more than

171
00:05:46,105 --> 00:05:47,743
seven percent.

172
00:05:47,883 --> 00:05:49,703
His kids have moved out.

173
00:05:49,703 --> 00:05:51,333
The house is too big.

174
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:53,073
And he stays anyway.

175
00:05:53,133 --> 00:05:55,893
Economists call it the lock-in effect.

176
00:05:55,893 --> 00:05:57,921
The golden handcuffs.

177
00:05:58,183 --> 00:06:00,815
Economists at the Federal Housing Finance

178
00:06:00,815 --> 00:06:02,833
Agency measured it.

179
00:06:02,963 --> 00:06:05,664
For every percentage point the market rate

180
00:06:05,664 --> 00:06:07,892
sits above a homeowner's own rate,

181
00:06:07,892 --> 00:06:09,693
the chance they sell falls by about

182
00:06:09,693 --> 00:06:11,083
eighteen percent.

183
00:06:11,083 --> 00:06:13,715
Between mid twenty twenty-two and the end

184
00:06:13,715 --> 00:06:15,367
of twenty twenty-three,

185
00:06:15,367 --> 00:06:18,478
they estimated lock-in prevented about one

186
00:06:18,478 --> 00:06:20,753
point three million home sales.

187
00:06:21,103 --> 00:06:23,705
And by keeping homes off the market,

188
00:06:23,705 --> 00:06:25,854
it pushed prices up by five point

189
00:06:25,854 --> 00:06:27,024
seven percent,

190
00:06:27,024 --> 00:06:30,011
more than higher rates pulled them down.

191
00:06:30,250 --> 00:06:32,112
When nobody moves,

192
00:06:32,112 --> 00:06:35,140
nothing opens up for the next buyer.

193
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:37,823
The share of Americans who moved in

194
00:06:37,823 --> 00:06:39,586
a year fell to a record low

195
00:06:39,586 --> 00:06:42,009
of eleven point two percent in twenty

196
00:06:42,009 --> 00:06:43,270
twenty-four.

197
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:46,176
Harvard says existing-home sales were

198
00:06:46,176 --> 00:06:48,141
stuck at four point one million in

199
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:49,590
twenty twenty-five,

200
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:50,794
a thirty-year low,

201
00:06:50,794 --> 00:06:52,759
two thirds of the pace of twenty

202
00:06:52,759 --> 00:06:53,850
twenty-one.

203
00:06:54,070 --> 00:06:55,462
And in October,

204
00:06:55,462 --> 00:06:58,058
the National Association of Realtors

205
00:06:58,058 --> 00:07:00,418
measured an annual pace of just three

206
00:07:00,418 --> 00:07:02,434
point seven five million.

207
00:07:06,883 --> 00:07:09,893
Then there's the oldest problem of all.

208
00:07:09,893 --> 00:07:11,273
Supply.

209
00:07:11,323 --> 00:07:13,114
After the housing crash,

210
00:07:13,114 --> 00:07:14,753
builders went quiet.

211
00:07:14,773 --> 00:07:16,722
By our count of Census data,

212
00:07:16,722 --> 00:07:19,788
America completed about fifteen and a half

213
00:07:19,788 --> 00:07:22,176
million new homes in the two thousands,

214
00:07:22,176 --> 00:07:24,366
but only about nine million in the

215
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:25,623
twenty-tens.

216
00:07:25,733 --> 00:07:27,286
In twenty eleven,

217
00:07:27,286 --> 00:07:29,088
just five hundred and eighty-five

218
00:07:29,088 --> 00:07:29,740
thousand,

219
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:31,992
the lowest year since the records began

220
00:07:31,992 --> 00:07:33,943
in nineteen sixty-eight.

221
00:07:34,183 --> 00:07:36,743
So how many homes are missing?

222
00:07:36,963 --> 00:07:37,721
Honestly,

223
00:07:37,721 --> 00:07:39,255
nobody knows exactly,

224
00:07:39,255 --> 00:07:41,603
and the estimates disagree.

225
00:07:41,913 --> 00:07:44,704
Harvard says various organizations put the

226
00:07:44,704 --> 00:07:46,797
shortage between one point two and five

227
00:07:46,797 --> 00:07:48,793
point five million homes.

228
00:07:48,943 --> 00:07:51,320
The research group Up for Growth estimates

229
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,433
about three point eight million.

230
00:07:53,553 --> 00:07:55,384
These are estimates built on different

231
00:07:55,384 --> 00:07:56,483
assumptions.

232
00:07:56,553 --> 00:07:58,265
But nearly all of them point the

233
00:07:58,265 --> 00:07:59,223
same way.

234
00:07:59,223 --> 00:08:00,673
Not enough.

235
00:08:00,917 --> 00:08:04,077
Let me sketch why the estimates differ.

236
00:08:04,077 --> 00:08:06,837
Box one is the homes we have.

237
00:08:06,837 --> 00:08:08,714
Box two is the homes we'd need,

238
00:08:08,714 --> 00:08:10,590
for every new household,

239
00:08:10,590 --> 00:08:12,910
plus a healthy number of empty homes

240
00:08:12,910 --> 00:08:15,197
so people can actually move.

241
00:08:15,377 --> 00:08:18,157
The arrow between them is the gap.

242
00:08:18,157 --> 00:08:19,721
Change the assumptions,

243
00:08:19,721 --> 00:08:22,097
and the gap stretches or shrinks.

244
00:08:22,097 --> 00:08:24,173
It never disappears.

245
00:08:24,417 --> 00:08:27,067
And building got more expensive.

246
00:08:27,067 --> 00:08:29,157
Harvard says the price of residential

247
00:08:29,157 --> 00:08:31,455
construction inputs jumped forty percent

248
00:08:31,455 --> 00:08:33,727
since January twenty twenty.

249
00:08:33,967 --> 00:08:35,807
The home builders' own trade group,

250
00:08:35,807 --> 00:08:36,483
the NAHB,

251
00:08:36,483 --> 00:08:39,278
estimates that regulation makes up

252
00:08:39,278 --> 00:08:41,607
twenty-six point four percent of the price

253
00:08:41,607 --> 00:08:43,913
of a new single-family home,

254
00:08:43,913 --> 00:08:46,475
about about one hundred and thirty-two

255
00:08:46,475 --> 00:08:48,007
thousand dollars.

256
00:08:48,077 --> 00:08:49,977
That's the builders' estimate.

257
00:08:49,977 --> 00:08:52,255
It's also a clue about where to

258
00:08:52,255 --> 00:08:53,361
look next.

259
00:08:57,817 --> 00:09:00,042
Welcome to the most powerful room in

260
00:09:00,042 --> 00:09:01,977
American housing.

261
00:09:01,997 --> 00:09:03,127
Not a bank.

262
00:09:03,127 --> 00:09:04,637
Not Congress.

263
00:09:04,647 --> 00:09:06,160
The local zoning board,

264
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:07,857
on a Tuesday night.

265
00:09:07,927 --> 00:09:09,996
Zoning decides what may be built on

266
00:09:09,996 --> 00:09:11,837
every piece of land.

267
00:09:11,917 --> 00:09:13,567
And in much of the country,

268
00:09:13,567 --> 00:09:16,047
the answer for homes is simple.

269
00:09:16,207 --> 00:09:17,220
One house,

270
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:18,868
on one lot.

271
00:09:19,117 --> 00:09:20,207
How much?

272
00:09:20,207 --> 00:09:22,709
Researchers at UC Berkeley mapped the Los

273
00:09:22,709 --> 00:09:24,795
Angeles region and found that about

274
00:09:24,795 --> 00:09:27,089
seventy-eight percent of its residential

275
00:09:27,089 --> 00:09:29,800
land is reserved for single-family homes

276
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:30,817
only.

277
00:09:31,267 --> 00:09:32,296
That's one region,

278
00:09:32,296 --> 00:09:34,157
not the whole country.

279
00:09:34,217 --> 00:09:35,726
But it shows how much land a

280
00:09:35,726 --> 00:09:37,835
single rule can cover.

281
00:09:38,083 --> 00:09:40,619
Want to put four apartments where one

282
00:09:40,619 --> 00:09:41,733
house stands?

283
00:09:41,733 --> 00:09:43,245
You may need a variance,

284
00:09:43,245 --> 00:09:44,100
a hearing,

285
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:46,673
and a room full of worried neighbors.

286
00:09:46,693 --> 00:09:47,759
Parking rules,

287
00:09:47,759 --> 00:09:48,608
height limits,

288
00:09:48,608 --> 00:09:50,513
minimum lot sizes.

289
00:09:50,553 --> 00:09:52,322
Each rule may make sense on its

290
00:09:52,322 --> 00:09:53,103
own.

291
